        "title": "2. BCD Code (+6 Rule) & Excess-3 Self-Complementing Magic",
        "content": "### 1. Binary Coded Decimal (BCD) & The +6 Correction Rule\n\n#### 8421 BCD (Natural BCD) Encoding:\nIn 8421 BCD, each decimal digit ($0$ through $9$) is encoded as an independent 4-bit nibble using weights $8, 4, 2, 1$:\n$$0_{10} = 0000_{\\text{BCD}}, \   \... \   9_{10} = 1001_{\\text{BCD}}$$\n- A 4-bit register has $2^4 = 16$ possible binary states ($0000_2$ to $1111_2$).\n- The 6 code words from **$1010_2$ ($10_{10}$) through $1111_2$ ($15_{10}$) are ILLEGAL / FORBIDDEN states**!\n\n---\n\n### 2. Analytical Reason for the +6 Correction in BCD Addition\n\nWhen two BCD digits are added using binary adders:\n1. **Condition for Correction:** If the 4-bit binary sum exceeds $9$ ($> 1001_2$) OR generates an auxiliary carry out of the nibble ($C_{\\text{out}} = 1$), the sum has stepped into or through the forbidden zone!\n2. **The +6 Rule:** The difference between hexadecimal radix ($16$) and decimal radix ($10$) is $16 - 10 = 6$. Adding binary $0110_2$ ($+6$) skips directly past the 6 invalid states, forcing the correct decimal carry into the next higher nibble:\n   $$\\mathbf{\\text{BCD Sum} = (\\text{Binary Sum}) + 0110_2 \   (\\text{if sum } > 9 \\text{ or carry } = 1)}$$\n\n---\n\n### 3. Excess-3 (XS-3) Code & Self-Complementing Property\n\nExcess-3 is an unweighted code obtained by adding binary $0011_2$ ($+3$) to each BCD digit:\n$$\\mathbf{\\text{XS-3} = \\text{BCD} + 0011_2}$$\n\n#### The Self-Complementing Theorem:\n> 🟢 **Theorem:** If an Excess-3 code word is bitwise inverted (taking 1's complement), the resulting code word is identical to the Excess-3 representation of the **9's complement** of the original decimal digit!\n> $$\\mathbf{\\overline{\\text{XS-3}(d)} = \\text{XS-3}(9 - d)}$$\n- **Engineering Advantage:** Allows digital arithmetic units to perform decimal subtraction using standard adders and simple inverter gates without complex borrow-propagation circuitry!\n\n---\n\n---\n\n\n**Problem:**\nConvert the decimal number $(47)_{10}$ into: (1) 8421 BCD code, (2) Excess-3 code.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: 8421 BCD (Convert each digit individually into 4 bits)**\n- $4 = 0100_2$\n- $7 = 0111_2$\n$$(47)_{10} = \\mathbf{0100 \; 0111_{\\text{BCD}}}$$\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Excess-3 Code (Add 3 to each digit)**\n- For digit $4$: $4 + 3 = 7 = 0111_2$\n- For digit $7$: $7 + 3 = 10 = 1010_2$\n$$(47)_{10} = \\mathbf{0111 \; 1010_{\\text{XS-3}}}$$\n\n\n🎯 $(47)_{10} = \\mathbf{0100 \; 0111_{\\text{BCD}}} = \\mathbf{0111 \; 1010_{\\text{XS-3}}}$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (6 Marks):**\nPerform the addition (38)_{BCD} + (45)_{BCD} using BCD arithmetic.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Binary Addition of Nibbles**\n- 38 = 0011 \; 1000\n- 45 = 0100 \; 0101\n\\begin{array}{r@{\  }l}\n  0011 & 1000 \\\\\\n+ 0100 & 0101 \\\\\\n\\hline\n  0111 & 1101 \   (7  and  13)\n\\end{array}\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Check for Illegal Nibbles (> 9)**\n- Lower nibble is 1101_2 = 13 > 9 ⇒ **INVALID BCD!**\n- Higher nibble is 0111_2 = 7 ≤ 9 ⇒ Valid.\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Add +6 (0110_2) to Lower Nibble**\n\\begin{array}{r@{\  }l}\n  0111 & 1101 \\\\\\n+ 0000 & 0110 \\\\\\n\\hline\n  1000 & 0011 \   (8  and  3)\n\\end{array}\n1000_2 = 8 and 0011_2 = 3 ⇒ (83)_(10). Matches 38 + 45 = 83!\n\n\n🎯 BCD Sum: $\\mathbf{1000 \; 0011_{\\text{BCD}} = (83)_{10}}$.\n\n\n\n\n**University Question (8 Marks):**\nDemonstrate the self-complementing property of Excess-3 code by finding the 9's complement of (62)_(10) using bitwise complementation in Excess-3.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 🚀: Represent (62)_(10) in Excess-3**\n- 6 + 3 = 9 = 1001_2\n- 2 + 3 = 5 = 0101_2\n(62)_(10) = 1001 \; 0101XS-3\n\n**Step 2 ⚡: Take Bitwise 1's Complement (Flip Every Bit)**\n\\overline{1001 \; 0101} = 0110 \; 1010_2\n\n**Step 3 🎯: Decode Back to Decimal by Subtracting 3**\n- Higher nibble: 0110_2 = 6 ⇒ 6 - 3 = 3\n- Lower nibble: 1010_2 = 10 ⇒ 10 - 3 = 7\nDecoded number is 37₁₀.\n\n**Step 4 🏁: Mathematical Verification**\nThe 9's complement of 62 is:\n99 - 62 = 37₁₀\nThe bitwise NOT gate directly executed decimal 9's complement subtraction with zero arithmetic!\n\n\n🎯 Proved: Excess-3 bitwise flip yields $0110 \; 1010_{\\text{XS-3}} = \\mathbf{37_{10}}$, exactly the 9's complement of $62$."
